WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.824
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.020
SPEAKER: Hello.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.800
Let's talk about API
program governance.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.940
What is governance,
and more importantly,

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.820
how would that apply
to your API program?

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.360
Governance is defined
as to rule and control.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.890
But in our fast
paced environment

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.690
where innovation
is key to attaining

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.060
that competitive
edge, heavy ruling

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.410
can control or slow down things.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:30.220
And slowing things down,
progress can be impeded.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.560
Governance when applied
to the API first model

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.400
is a balancing act.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.310
We need to manage
risk while enabling

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.740
speed and agility in order
to meet the consumer's needs.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.020
We don't want heavyweight
authority, control,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.680
or regulation, and this is
why we call it a little g.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.380
Finding the balance depends
on your organization:

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.530
startup versus traditional
enterprise, risk appetite

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.190
versus speed requirement.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.320
Let's talk about the
keys to managing risk

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.150
while still maintaining the
ability to meet market needs.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.980
The balance comes from an
intersection of 4 components.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.660
First are the key roles
within an API team.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.180
Second is the structure
of the team focused

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.660
on the digital initiative.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.930
Third is the ownership
of the API products,

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:18.670
and the fourth and
final component

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.490
are the simple guidelines and
structures around the platform

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.050
for ease of adoption.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.510
As mentioned in the previous
video around API team

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.720
composition, it is essential
to have an API team

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.960
leader who is aligned with
both the business and IT.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.270
This role is called
the API product owner,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.220
and it is a key
component to ensuring

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.000
that the APIs built target
a priority business outcome.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.790
Central team defines API
development standards

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:46.450
and developed APIs based on
the requirements from product,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.490
project, and
business unit teams.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.160
This model allows for
consistency and development,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.200
approach, and adherence.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.540
However, this model
lacks the agility

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.760
that some of the
other models have.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.390
A decentralized operating model
has the agility and the speed,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.400
but lacks the standards
and common development

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.960
best practices.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.120
This combined with a
lack of clear ownership

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.490
after the APIs are
released showcases

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.650
how different this model
is compared to that

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.300
of the centralized model.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.060
So now we arrive at
the hybrid model.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.370
This model possesses
the speed and agility

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.070
of the decentralized model
while also maintaining

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.690
a close adherence to standards.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.790
The downside is that
a lot of coordination

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.860
is needed when it comes
to the requirements

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.590
across multiple teams.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.210
So who owns which APIs?

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.550
Ownership of the APIs,
or the components

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.520
that make up those APIs, is
dependent upon the operating

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.080
model.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.230
For example, in a hybrid
model, the platform team

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.840
is responsible for setting
guidelines, standards,

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.270
and the reusable assets.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.810
Reusable assets are in the
form of shared flows or shared

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.590
services that adhere to a common
practice such as security,

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.320
traffic management,
or air handling.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.050
This frees up the
specific product, project,

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.170
and business unit teams
to focus on API supporting

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.600
their business objectives.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.090
Delivery can be much
faster when the teams don't

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.400
have to worry about the
standards for common assets.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.340
Reusable assets can be
selected from an a la carte

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.120
menu to include in the
delivery team's API product.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.562
Big G Governance is what
most companies end up with.

00:03:21.562 --> 00:03:23.020
This assumes that
the teams are not

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.770
trusted to do the right thing,
and result in heavy gating.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.360
Committees like
change approval boards

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.850
are formed to manually
audit any change that

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.320
are pushed to deployed systems.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.710
Policies and procedures are
written into heavyweight Word

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.650
documents, and teams are split
by function, as they are not

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.630
trusted to work together.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.480
Little g governance is harder
for an organization to swallow,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.910
as responsibility
and accountability

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.510
is pushed down to where the
decision needs to be made.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.220
It provides enough
governance to keep

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:55.880
the teams on the right path, but
doesn't seek to slow delivery.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.700
Now let's run through
an analogy of a freeway.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.530
While you're on a
freeway, the direction

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.710
is set, but can be
changed at junctions.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.440
Guard rails helped keep cars
on the right side of the road

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.540
and traveling safely
in the right direction.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.180
Lanes are painted to suggest
the right way to drive,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.580
but can be crossed if needed.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.279
The emphasis here is
on keeping the flow.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:20.579
Now imagine if big
government was in control.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:23.850
Every time you needed to
accelerate, slow down, change

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.510
lanes, or even change
direction, someone else

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.260
would need to have to
review that decision

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.910
and approve it for you.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.220
Now imagine what that
would do to traffic.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.950
Little g governance provide
standards principles

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.690
and best practices.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.940
Teams are trusted and empowered
through tooling and automation.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.840
Information and documentation
is shared collaboratively.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.420
Teams are cross-functional
to minimize handoff

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.460
and communication issues.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.230
Going back to the
freeway analogy,

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:55.270
little g governance operates
how we think of freeways today.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.400
Organizations have hierarchy.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:01.090
What tends to happen is that we
push information to authority.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:04.130
The people at the bottom
have the information,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.940
but they don't have the
authority to make the decision.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.580
This results in
bottlenecks and delays.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.890
We create systems that
channel information up,

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.840
where decision is
then taken, and then

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.030
that information
comes back down again.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.150
As you can imagine, this
tends to take a lot of time.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.280
And alternative is pushing
authority to the information.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.470
The foundation for this
model is making sure

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.080
that the people that are
closest to the information

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.330
are both technically
competent and have

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.790
organizational clarity.

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.530
They understand the
priorities and know

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.180
what the right thing to do.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.800
They are empowered to make the
decisions, and as a result,

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.410
they are happier for it.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.510
If your organization
is truly undergoing

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.760
a digital transformation,
it is deeper

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.100
than building a mobile
app or putting some APIs

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.033
on a developer portal.

00:05:49.033 --> 00:05:51.200
Think about why your company
started this initiative

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:52.890
in the first place.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.790
If the benefits include reducing
costs and accelerating time

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.680
to market, then
the initiative has

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.920
to go well beyond that
of just building APIs.

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.680
To achieve those aims,
imposing a heavy weight process

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.260
that encourages blind
compliance with no room

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.800
to accelerate delivery will
at best slow, or at worst,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.110
stop the benefits
of the program.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.900
To achieve those aims,
imposing a heavy weight process

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.510
that encourages blind
compliance with no room

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.080
to accelerate delivery will
at best slow, or at worst,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.450
stop the benefits
of the program.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:25.400
Much of the time
governance, processes

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.460
are imposed top down, and
borne out of quick resolutions

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.020
to problems encountered.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.940
The gates imposed often
have documentation

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.560
required at each
stage, which are

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.680
slow to produce, and discourage
effective communication

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:38.470
between teams.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.320
Ultimately, the focus is
on the process protecting

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.320
the organization from the
IT teams, who they don't

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.550
trust to do the right thing.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:49.330
This is done so rather than
accelerating business outcomes

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:50.660
in a safe manner.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:53.990
So how do we apply this to
the API governance model?

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.240
If a centralized team
have all the authority,

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.850
then we can't scale, and
agility is inhibited.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:03.270
We must give developers,
publishing or consuming APIs,

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:04.860
the information
and knowledge they

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.770
need to publish and use
those APIs thank you.

